      <!-- SECTION 4 -->
      <section id="testing-documentation">
        <h2>Testing and Documentation Requirements</h2>
        <p>
          VOC content compliance under all major frameworks is established by calculation or analytical testing, with documentation held at the manufacturer level for inspection. There is no pre-market approval or registration authority to submit compliance to under EU Directive 2004/42/EC — it is a self-declaration framework. US EPA and CARB are similarly self-declaration systems without a central registration, but regulatory agencies conduct market surveillance and can request compliance records during inspections or enforcement actions. Accurate documentation is therefore a business-critical requirement, not just good practice.
        </p>
        <p>
          For <strong>EU Directive 2004/42/EC</strong>, VOC content is measured using ISO 11890-1 (gravimetric method for VOC content greater than approximately 15%) or ISO 11890-2 (GC-based method for lower VOC content). Alternatively, calculation from the formulation composition using known or supplier-declared VOC content values for each ingredient is accepted where the analytical measurement is not practical, provided the calculation approach is documented and defensible. The product's technical documentation must include the product category classification rationale, the VOC content value, the measurement or calculation method, and the applicable Phase II limit for the category. Labelling must indicate the VOC content category in the range descriptors specified by the Directive (e.g., "Very Low: <1 g/L", "Low: <30 g/L", etc.) for relevant categories.
        </p>
        <p>
          For <strong>US EPA and CARB compliance</strong>, the regulatory VOC is calculated using EPA Method 24 (for waterborne coatings) or ASTM D2369 (standard test method for VOC content), with water and exempt compounds excluded from both numerator and denominator. Manufacturers must maintain records identifying the product category, the VOC calculation method, the identity and quantity of any exempt compounds claimed, and the regulatory basis for each exemption. CARB's <a href="https://www.epa.gov/stationary-sources-air-pollution/paint-varnish-and-lacquer-coatings" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">enforcement programme</a> conducts periodic market sampling and product testing, and misclassification or unsupported exempt compound claims are the most frequent sources of enforcement action.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 5 -->
      <section id="certification-pathway">
        <h2>Step-by-Step Compliance Pathway for VOC Regulations in Coatings</h2>
        <p>
          VOC compliance for coating products follows a logical sequence from product classification through formulation validation to documentation and labelling. The steps below apply to both EU Directive 2004/42/EC and US EPA/CARB compliance, with framework-specific notes where the process diverges. Begin this process at the formulation design stage, not after the product is ready for market — reformulation after a non-compliance finding is expensive and delays market entry.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Classify the product.</strong> Identify which regulatory category your product falls into under each target market framework — EU Directive 2004/42/EC Annex I product type, and the applicable US EPA AIM or CARB product category. Category misclassification is the most common single source of compliance error. When the product could plausibly fit multiple categories, document the rationale for the chosen classification.</li>
          <li><strong>Determine the applicable VOC limit.</strong> Look up the Phase II limit (EU) or CARB/EPA limit (US) for the classified product category. Note that limits differ between jurisdictions and between product categories — do not assume that the limit for a similar product type in another framework applies.</li>
          <li><strong>Calculate or measure the VOC content.</strong> For EU: use ISO 11890-1 or -2, or calculate from formulation composition using supplier VOC data. For US: use EPA Method 24 or ASTM D2369 excluding water and documented exempt compounds from both numerator and denominator.</li>
          <li><strong>Assess compliance and reformulate if required.</strong> If the VOC content exceeds the limit for any target market, reformulate — typical strategies include increasing water content (waterborne systems), replacing regulated solvents with low-VOC or exempt alternatives, increasing resin solids content (high-solids approach), or shifting to a reactive or UV-cure system where the chemistry permits.</li>
          <li><strong>Re-test and document the compliant formulation.</strong> After reformulation, repeat the VOC measurement or calculation for the final formulation. Document all evidence: formulation composition, VOC calculation, measurement test report, product category, and the applicable regulatory limit. Update the product information file and any Safety Data Sheets affected by the formulation change.</li>
          <li><strong>Label the product correctly.</strong> Apply VOC content information per the applicable labelling requirements — EU Directive VOC content category descriptor on the label; appropriate VOC statements on US product labels and SDS per GHS/CLP requirements.</li>
          <li><strong>Monitor for regulatory changes.</strong> VOC limits are updated periodically — CARB has revised its architectural coatings limits multiple times since 2007, and the EU Directive 2004/42/EC is subject to review. Assign ongoing responsibility for regulatory monitoring to a named individual or team so formulation reassessment is triggered promptly when limits change.</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Key Compliance Principle</strong>
          The measurement method is as important as the numeric limit. Calculating regulatory VOC under EU methods (including water in the denominator) versus US CARB methods (excluding water and exempt compounds from both numerator and denominator) produces different numbers for the same product. Always specify which method and which framework's definition you are applying when recording a VOC compliance value — mixing methods invalidates the compliance assessment entirely.
        </div>
      </section>

